      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50548119",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Modern GPU load/store units (LSUs) dictate kernel execution speed, yet their internal queueing and pipeline contention mechanics have long operated as black boxes. Recent reverse-engineering of modern NVIDIA microarchitectures has produced cycle-accurate latency equations for global L1 hits and shared memory lookups, exposing that bank conflicts and L1 tag lookups create measurable contention across workloads.\n\nWhen the LSU pipeline fills completely, active warps can indefinitely starve warps launched from subsequent thread blocks. In multi-tenant environments using NVIDIA Multi-Process Service (MPS), this hardware bottleneck breaches security boundaries: bank access patterns and tag lookups provide side-channel timing signals fast enough to extract full AES-128 keys across co-located processes without direct memory access.\n\nFor engineers designing shared GPU clusters and multi-tenant ML inference infrastructure, hardware isolation at the CUDA runtime level is insufficient. Cross-workload cache interference directly impacts predictable latency SLAs and introduces covert data exfiltration paths on shared silicon."
    },
    {
