      "content": "Running concurrent coding agents against a local Ollama instance can unexpectedly collapse inference performance. When two independent agent sessions alternate turns on a single server slot, median time-to-first-token spikes from one second up to forty-six seconds.\n\nThis slowdown is not primarily raw compute contention. It is a severe cache invalidation problem. Each agent turn submits an increasingly long prompt that shares an identical system prompt, tool definitions, and historical context. When sessions share a single inference slot, the engine constantly evicts attention key-value caches to process the incoming interleaved request. In empirical tests, fifty-four percent of all prompt tokens had to be recalculated repeatedly on a shared slot, compared to just 0.4 percent when assigning dedicated slots per session.\n\nProviding dedicated execution slots eliminates cache churn and restores prompt prefix matching. Local inference harnesses must align slot allocation policies directly with agent session lifecycles to prevent self-inflicted prompt processing bottlenecks.\n\nIn multi-agent infrastructure, memory locality matters far more than raw model concurrency."
    },
