      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50549935",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Mapping POSIX file operations onto object storage has long been a notorious distributed systems challenge due to high latency, append limits, and strict consistency constraints. FiberFS introduces an architectural pattern that achieves distributed POSIX compliance directly against S3-compatible APIs without intermediate database coordination.\n\nRather than relying on external coordination clusters or proprietary metadata stores, FiberFS maintains directory trees and file metadata as standalone JSON objects directly within the S3 bucket. Concurrency and conflict resolution across multiple client instances rely entirely on native S3 HTTP conditionals, ensuring synchronized, versioned state transitions across concurrent writers.\n\nTo overcome latency bottlenecks inherent in object storage round trips, the architecture implements four cascading caching layers: local page cache, local disk cache, clustered cache, and CDN cache. The system also supports write-through caching, allowing immediate read-after-write operations without incurring S3 network round trips.\n\nBuilding POSIX semantics directly on commodity object storage offers an elegant infrastructure blueprint for scalable shared storage."
