      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand how attackers weaponized .pth files in malicious PyPI packages to exfiltrate CI/CD tokens on installation without requiring an explicit import.",
      "hn_id": "50543774",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50543774",
      "image": null,
      "content": "A supply chain attack against Microsoft's official agent-framework metapackage demonstrates how dangerous blind package installations remain in modern CI/CD pipelines.\n\nThe compromised releases did not require a script to explicitly import the module. Instead, they embedded a .pth file that automatically ran arbitrary Python code whenever the interpreter initialized. A simple pip install executed the payload during environment setup.\n\nOnce active, the script extracted local environment variables, mined credential stores, and queried internal metadata endpoints from Azure DevOps, Azure managed identity, and GitHub Actions for short-lived tokens. Even after the initial release was yanked from PyPI, the attackers uploaded a secondary variant under altered filenames within minutes.\n\nThis incident highlights a glaring blind spot in standard containerized build pipelines. Pinning exact package hashes and isolating build-time network egress must be treated as baseline operational safeguards rather than optional hardening."
    },
    {
