      "content": "Reasoning models spend immense token budgets on chain-of-thought sequences to solve complex programming tasks. In interactive coding agent harnesses, this latency and cost accumulate rapidly across multi-step execution loops.\n\nA new reinforcement-learning-trained LoRA adapter for Qwen3.8-27B demonstrates that extensive thinking tokens are not strictly necessary to achieve high-accuracy tool execution. When tested inside the pi coding agent harness, the adapter matched the base model running at maximum reasoning effort on SWE-bench Pro at 64.7 percent accuracy, while generating 4.5 times fewer tokens per task.\n\nRather than wasting turns rambling through verbose internal reasoning blocks, the adapted model navigates file trees, applies focused diffs, and runs validation tests in under half the turns. On SWE-bench Pro, the base model frequently exhaust-searched until hitting the 100-step limit, whereas the adapted weights resolved identical instances within eight tool calls.\n\nOptimizing models directly for agent harness interactions provides a compelling alternative to scaling test-time compute. Aligning the weights directly to tool calling saves both runtime latency and inference costs."
