      "content": "Coding agents frequently make poor decisions because standard semantic search retrieves outdated documentation alongside current design specifications. Dense retrieval and BM25 rank documents by keyword or semantic similarity, remaining completely oblivious to temporal supersession.\n\nLEKS benchmarks reveal a practical architectural approach to this problem by explicitly tracking replaced notes and superseded specifications. In empirical evaluations across 695 questions, this mechanism surfaces the newer specification above the deprecated one in over 70 percent of pairs, compared to less than 25 percent for standard retrieval methods.\n\nFurthermore, the system manages to retain 76.3 percent retrieval accuracy relying purely on word matching without running embedding models, completing queries in roughly 6 milliseconds while consuming just 63 megabytes of memory. This provides an effective local alternative to heavy vector databases for developer agent harnesses.\n\nContext retrieval for agents should account for time and state changes rather than treating text collections as static corpora."
    },
    {
