      "content": "Current coding agent benchmarks evaluate models against static issue descriptions where complete context is neatly handed over. The SWE-CoWork benchmark tests a much tougher reality by placing agents inside simulated organizations with 26 distinct human personas across 101 production repositories.\n\nThe findings are illuminating for anyone attempting to deploy autonomous engineers into real teams. While top models satisfy roughly 63 percent of fail-to-pass unit tests, they successfully complete fewer than 8 percent of actual end-to-end assignments. Most failures stem from poor requirement recovery and an inability to reconcile conflicting information across distributed colleague records.\n\nProviding complete requirements upfront raised unit test pass rates by 16 percentage points, yet total project completion remained stubbornly flat. The bottleneck in real-world software engineering is rarely raw code generation; it is discovering implicit constraints and verifying cross-service side effects.\n\nBuilding dependable agents requires prioritizing context elicitation and iterative multi-party consensus over raw code synthesis."
