      "content": "Patching an unsafe architecture with kernel filters will never turn an insecure runtime into a sandbox. When Telegram introduced embedded mini-apps inside chat messages, security researchers found a remote code execution vulnerability within twenty-four hours. The engineering team deployed a seccomp filter to plug the hole, only for researchers to bypass that filter two days later.\n\nThe fundamental breakdown was architectural rather than algorithmic. The chat application attempted to execute arbitrary JavaScript within the same memory and process boundaries that managed user sessions, private encryption keys, and active network connections. An attacker who gains memory corruption inside the JavaScript engine immediately inherits the privileges of the surrounding application.\n\nHardening this design requires treating the execution engine as already compromised. Production-grade isolation places each widget in an unprivileged child process, bounds execution with software fault isolation, and validates all inter-process communication as untrusted input. A seccomp allowlist can only restrict system calls; it cannot fix architectural vulnerabilities in the process boundary itself.\n\nTrue isolation is built into the process boundary, not bolted on after the crash."
