      "image": null,
      "content": "Exposing programmatic tools directly to browser agents creates hidden architectural bypasses that violate user consent.\n\nWhen an agent uses WebMCP to invoke an in-page tool callback, it completely skips visual friction gates like surge pricing warnings or modal confirmations. The frontend team assumes every business action traverses the user interface, while the backend team assumes any request originating from an authenticated browser session carries informed user consent.\n\nThis architectural disconnect leads to unintentional dark patterns and unauthorized financial commitments. In a ride-hailing scenario, dynamic pricing can suddenly spike a fare from twenty-five to eighty-five dollars. The web interface locks the purchase button behind an explicit confirmation dialogue, yet a co-located browser assistant invokes the booking callback directly.\n\nExposing an in-page API without replicating interface confirmation states creates silent liabilities for production systems."
