      "image": null,
      "content": "Passing an upstream OAuth token directly into a Model Context Protocol server creates a severe security vulnerability.\n\nMany developers treat authorization as a simple pass-through mechanism, allowing an agent to reuse a third-party token like a GitHub access key across downstream MCP servers. This practice collapses distinct trust boundaries into token teleportation, violating the core specifications of both OAuth 2.1 and MCP itself.\n\nThe 2025 MCP authorization standard explicitly defines every MCP server as an independent protected resource. Clients must discover metadata dynamically, enforce Proof Key for Code Exchange (PKCE), and negotiate audience-restricted tokens scoped strictly for that specific MCP instance. Furthermore, coarse OAuth scopes only validate client identity; they cannot evaluate whether an agent should execute a destructive tool call after reading untrusted input.\n\nRobust agent security demands audience-bound authentication at the connection layer and fine-grained authorization policies at the tool-call layer."
